  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 103 AM MDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nA low pressure system over Oregon will produce showers and \nstorms over our area into the weekend. This system has tapped \ninto monsoon moisture and lifted it northward. We are seeing \nshowers and storms move north out of Utah and into central and \neast Idaho today. Occasional showers and storms will be the \ntheme for the next couple days as this low slowly moves east. We\nprobably will not see activity completely end until Saturday \nnight when the low moves east. A marginal risk of severe storms \nexists both Today and Friday with strong outflow winds possible \nin the more intense storms. Rainfall amounts are heavily \ndependent upon where thunderstorms develop and lay down heavy \ndownpours. The eastern highlands should see the best rainfall \namounts with 0.50 to 0.75 inches while most of central Idaho \nwill only see amounts of a few hundredths to perhaps 0.25 inches\nin the mountains. \n\nWe should see dry weather return for Sunday and beyond. We're not as \nconcerned about frost in the Snake River Plain in the wake of this \nsystem as temps have been steadily rising as newer model solutions \ncome in. Another system will enter the Great Basin early next week, \nbut it looks like it may stay far enough south that we may not see \nany impacts from it.",
